# 11. How to resolve the functional conflict

The three functions should be asked to stop defending their local explanations and jointly produce one order-level fact base.

A practical workshop would take 8–10 recent orders, including:

- Two delivered on time;
- Two late;
- Two with warranty or rework;
- Two expedited;
- Two representative standard orders.

For each order, the team should reconstruct the full timeline using dated system records, inspection logs, purchase orders, and customer correspondence.

The output should identify:

- What actually delayed the order;
- Which delays were predictable;
- Which delays were created by another function;
- Which delays were self-inflicted;
- Which controls are mandatory;
- Which delays can be removed immediately;
- Which problems require investment or design change.

The discussion should be evidence-led rather than organized around function-specific narratives.

The operating message can be stated simply:

> **Sales owns the customer promise, production owns flow, quality owns release integrity, and all three own the result.**

No function should be measured as successful when the customer receives a late, defective, or uneconomic product.

## Role responsibilities

| Role | Primary responsibilities |
|---|---|
| Executive sponsor | Remove organizational barriers, approve priorities, enforce shared accountability |
| Improvement leader | Own the roadmap, cadence, metrics, issue escalation, and benefits tracking |
| Sales/commercial | Capture complete requirements, use validated promise dates, control customer changes, report delivery-sensitive losses |
| Engineering | Define product class, release complete designs, maintain configuration rules, assess changes |
| Production planning | Maintain finite-capacity schedule, control frozen horizon, manage bottleneck sequence |
| Production | Follow standard work, identify blockers early, perform approved in-process checks, protect first-time quality |
| Quality | Maintain gate integrity, authorize qualified personnel, measure quality risk, audit the accelerated flow |
| Procurement/supply chain | Secure critical materials, manage supplier capacity and documentation, maintain approved alternatives |
| Customer service | Track approvals, communicate changes, assemble customer documentation, escalate blocked decisions |
| Finance | Validate margin, working-capital, expedite, and investment impacts |
| HR/training | Maintain competence records and cross-training plans for quality-critical roles |

Each action from the weekly council should include:

- One accountable owner;
- One supporting function;
- A due date;
- A measurable expected result;
- A status of green, amber, or red;
- An escalation date if overdue.

