# 17. Minimum operating standards

Every team, regardless of country or work mode, should meet a common minimum standard.

## Before a meeting

- State the purpose and required outcome;
- Identify who must attend;
- Circulate relevant materials in advance;
- Confirm whether the meeting could be handled asynchronously;
- Check time-zone and accessibility implications;
- Identify any confidential information.

## During a meeting

- Use reliable audio as the minimum requirement;
- Explain the participation method;
- Invite contributions from remote and less senior participants;
- Avoid side conversations;
- Clarify decisions and unresolved questions;
- Pause when language or connectivity creates difficulty;
- Record the meeting only where appropriate and lawful.

## After a meeting

- Record decisions, owners, and deadlines;
- Publish materials in the shared workspace;
- Identify people who need to be informed but did not attend;
- Escalate unresolved decisions through the agreed route;
- Avoid reopening settled decisions solely because some participants were physically present.

## For written collaboration

- Use agreed naming and filing conventions;
- Record material decisions in a searchable location;
- Use plain language where teams work across languages;
- Distinguish urgent matters from routine updates;
- Respect working hours and right-to-disconnect expectations;
- Avoid making private chat the sole location for important decisions.

