# 18. Suggested KPI scorecard

The company should establish a baseline before each pilot and compare results with a relevant pre-pilot period or control group where possible.

| Dimension | Sample KPI | Suggested review cadence |
|---|---|---|
| Business performance | Delivery reliability, quality defects, customer satisfaction, service-level attainment | Monthly or quarterly |
| Productivity | Cycle time, completed priority outcomes, rework, project predictability | Monthly |
| Collaboration | Decision turnaround time, meeting load, cross-location project health | Monthly |
| Employee experience | Clarity, trust, focus time, belonging, flexibility satisfaction | Quarterly |
| Well-being | Burnout risk, after-hours activity, absence, ergonomic incidents | Monthly or quarterly |
| Inclusion | Promotion, performance-rating, development, and senior-access gaps by work mode | Quarterly |
| Talent | Offer acceptance, retention, internal mobility, time to fill critical roles | Quarterly |
| Workplace | Occupancy by space type, collaboration-room utilization, employee purpose ratings | Monthly |
| Technology | Tool availability, support resolution time, adoption, security incidents | Monthly |
| Risk | Policy exceptions, cross-border violations, data incidents, health-and-safety events | Monthly |

The company should set explicit thresholds that trigger investigation. For example:

- A promotion-rate gap of more than 10 percentage points between comparable work modes;
- Repeated after-hours meetings affecting one region for three consecutive months;
- A material decline in early-career retention;
- More than 20% of recurring meetings rated as having no clear purpose;
- Office attendance rising without improvement in collaboration or delivery measures;
- Repeated security exceptions associated with home working;
- A significant increase in workload or burnout scores for a particular location or function.

These thresholds should trigger inquiry and corrective action, not automatic punishment or a return to a previous work model.

## 18.1 Measurement safeguards

Employee surveys and digital data should be governed by clear safeguards:

- Report results in aggregate;
- Set minimum sample sizes;
- Prohibit individual productivity scoring based solely on status or keystrokes;
- Explain what data is collected and why;
- Consult employee representatives where required;
- Separate support and well-being data from performance decisions;
- Retain data only for an approved period;
- Provide a route to challenge inaccurate data;
- Review metrics for cultural and language bias.

Metrics should help leaders understand how work is functioning. They should not recreate the visibility culture the framework is intended to replace.

