# 20. Change and communication plan

Employees should hear the same global rationale while receiving guidance that is relevant to their country and role.

## Enterprise communication

The company should explain:

- Why the framework is being introduced;
- What is global and non-negotiable;
- What can be decided locally or by teams;
- How eligibility decisions will be made;
- How employees can request review or accommodation;
- What data will be measured;
- When the policy will be reviewed;
- What will happen during the transition.

## Manager communication

Managers should receive:

- Country-specific guidance;
- Conversation scripts;
- Role-classification examples;
- Meeting and team-agreement templates;
- Escalation contacts;
- Training on accommodations and confidentiality;
- Examples of acceptable and unacceptable rationales;
- A checklist for reviewing team equity.

## Employee communication

Employees should have access to:

- A searchable policy hub;
- Country-level FAQs;
- Work-mode guidance by role;
- Approved-location information;
- Equipment and expense guidance;
- Collaboration standards;
- Appeal and exception instructions;
- Accessibility support;
- A confidential channel for concerns.

Communication should be available in relevant local languages and formats. Where consultation with works councils, unions, or employee representatives is required, it should occur before implementation rather than after the policy has effectively been decided.

