# 21. Risk register and mitigations

| Risk | Early warning sign | Mitigation | Owner |
|---|---|---|---|
| Proximity bias | Office-based employees receive more promotions or high-visibility work | Calibrate performance and promotion decisions; track access by work mode; train managers | HR and business leaders |
| Policy inconsistency | Comparable teams apply materially different rules without a business reason | Publish role criteria; conduct quarterly regional audits; require rationale for restrictions | HR |
| Loss of culture and belonging | Lower connection or belonging scores, especially among new hires | Structured connection events, onboarding, mentoring, and purposeful in-person gatherings | Business and HR |
| Decline in early-career learning | Slower ramp-up, lower junior retention, fewer development assignments | Apprenticeship, shadowing, manager check-ins, and scheduled learning cohorts | Talent and business leaders |
| Collaboration overload | Meeting hours and after-hours messaging increase | Meeting audits, asynchronous standards, focus-time protection, response-time norms | Team leaders |
| Time-zone inequity | The same region repeatedly receives inconvenient meetings | Rotate schedules, document decisions, use regional handoffs, monitor burden by location | Global team leaders |
| Cybersecurity or data leakage | Increased incidents, insecure networks, or unauthorized tools | Approved-device and location controls, zero-trust access, training, DLP, incident response | Information Security |
| Cross-border tax or immigration exposure | Unapproved work from another country or unclear employee location | Approved-location matrix, pre-travel approval, tax and immigration review, tracking | Legal, Tax, and HR |
| Health and safety failures | Ergonomic complaints, home-working injuries, stress or isolation | Home-work assessments, equipment support, safety guidance, manager check-ins | Health and Safety |
| Tool fragmentation | Duplicate platforms, lost decisions, poor searchability | Approved collaboration architecture, procurement controls, migration plan | Technology |
| Office underuse or poor fit | Low occupancy for focus and collaboration spaces, employee dissatisfaction | Rebalance space by purpose; test layouts; use occupancy data with qualitative feedback | Real Estate |
| Employee surveillance concerns | Distrust of monitoring or increased complaints | Transparency, data minimization, legal review, prohibition on keystroke-based productivity scoring | HR, Legal, and Privacy |
| Local cultural backlash | Low adoption, informal workarounds, reduced trust | Local consultation, translated guidance, culturally capable managers, regional pilots | Regional leadership |
| Flexibility limited to privileged groups | Site-based workers report fewer options or lower fairness | Expand flexibility to scheduling, shifts, leave, transport, job rotation, and predictable rosters | Operations and HR |
| Business continuity failure | Loss of critical capacity during disruption | Redundant locations, tested communication trees, cross-training, emergency work protocols | Business continuity |

The register should be reviewed at least quarterly and after any major policy, technology, security, or legal change.

