# 22. Responsibility model

The framework will succeed only if accountability is explicit.

## Executive leadership

- Set the strategic direction;
- Fund the required technology, workplace, and capability investments;
- Resolve trade-offs across cost, talent, risk, and employee experience;
- Hold leaders accountable for outcomes rather than symbolic attendance.

## Human Resources

- Own the global policy architecture;
- Maintain role-eligibility standards;
- Support accommodations and appeals;
- Monitor employee experience, equity, promotion, and retention;
- Provide manager capability programs;
- Coordinate local consultation.

## Business and functional leaders

- Define the outcomes and operational requirements of their functions;
- Approve work modes based on evidence;
- Ensure customer and service obligations are met;
- Sponsor pilots and act on results.

## People managers

- Set clear expectations;
- Create team operating agreements;
- Allocate development and high-visibility work fairly;
- Monitor workload and well-being;
- Explain attendance requirements;
- Avoid proximity bias;
- Escalate legal, security, or employee-relations concerns.

## Technology and Information Security

- Provide reliable, accessible collaboration infrastructure;
- Maintain approved tools and identity controls;
- Protect data across locations;
- Train employees in secure working practices;
- Monitor incidents without turning security controls into unnecessary surveillance.

## Legal, Tax, Privacy, and Employee Relations

- Review country-specific requirements;
- Maintain approved-location and cross-border guidance;
- Advise on monitoring, consultation, employment, and working-time issues;
- Ensure local adaptations remain lawful.

## Real Estate and Facilities

- Design offices around agreed purposes;
- Measure utilization by type and outcome;
- Provide accessible, safe, technology-enabled environments;
- Coordinate changes with affected employees and local leaders.

## Employees

- Follow security, confidentiality, health, and collaboration standards;
- Keep location and availability information accurate;
- Participate responsibly in team agreements;
- Raise accessibility, workload, safety, or inclusion concerns;
- Use flexibility in a way that supports agreed outcomes.

