# 23. Transition principles

During the transition, the company should avoid abrupt changes that create uncertainty without improving work.

It should:

- Honor existing commitments during defined transition periods;
- Give employees reasonable notice of material changes;
- Protect approved accommodations;
- Avoid changing office expectations at the same time as changing performance criteria unless both are clearly explained;
- Provide equipment and technology before requiring a new work pattern;
- Test new office layouts before committing to major capital expenditure;
- Preserve critical in-person activities while assessing alternatives;
- Communicate what is temporary, what is under review, and what is permanent;
- Review the effect of changes on employees who cannot work remotely.

The transition should be managed as an operating-model change, not as a real-estate announcement or a technology rollout.

